[thin] Specific fitted M3 insert-host interface: these condenser-finger samples span 2.000 mm from the purchased insert pilot to the outer finger boundary. The Ruthex RX-series table specifies a 4.0 mm pilot and 1.6 mm minimum surrounding wall for RX-M3x5.7. This retained local section meets that dimensional guidance; native correspondence identifies the pre-dock interface. It does not qualify installed pullout or finger load capacity.
alternatives: A larger host changes the donor-flange and driver-approach envelope. Keep the manufacturer-compatible pilot and host while qualifying actual insert retention and loaded finger performance.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-front-bottom.step
click: x=70.504 y=149.000 z=137.366
click: x=70.493 y=149.000 z=6.561

[ceiling] Unavoidable lifting interfaces: these Z29.25 handhold ceilings retain the flat finger bearings, shared R6 ends and 12 mm roof sections. Support can leave through the open handhold and seam before assembly. Actual cleanup, bare-surface comfort and loaded lifting remain unqualified.
alternatives: Sloping changes the finger bearing and opening. Filling below the roof blocks upward entry. Retain the specified working profile and assess its support contacts in the production slice.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-front-bottom.step
click: x=-99.479 y=190.000 z=29.250
click: x=99.479 y=190.000 z=29.250

[ceiling] Unavoidable square capture interfaces: these Z169.2 flats are the front rail catches. Their opposing feet retain the native 0.50 mm running/support allowance. The flat overlap resists lifting after that play is taken up. Support can leave the open rail lane before assembly; removal effort remains unqualified.
alternatives: Sloping introduces a camming bearing. Filling the lane enters foot travel; removing the overlap loses capture.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-front-bottom.step
click: x=-100.875 y=144.918 z=169.200
click: x=100.875 y=144.918 z=169.200

[slope] The linter is wrong for this channel: this 45° face is the roof of the relief over the lower Y-stack screw passage where it crosses front-bottom's knuckle underside (`_y_bore_reliefs`). It rises toward the passage inside a slot of the passage's own width, so both of its rising edges stand on the slot's walls 5.2 mm apart and each layer of it is carried at both ends as well as by the layer below; the rule reads one wall and its rising edge, a slope hanging sideways off a single wall.
alternatives: A level roof over the slot is a down-facing ceiling. A roof rising off the slot walls, a gable, dives under the 45° knuckle face along a V and leaves a ridge down the slot. A vertical end wall where the roof lands meets the knuckle face on a 45° arris. The rule could count a slope whose two rising edges stand on opposed walls as carried.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-front-bottom.step
click: x=0.000 y=211.432 z=57.948

[thin] Approved by Derek as drawn: each reading is a vent bar of the condenser vents on front-bottom's two flanks, the mullion between two slots struck down the floors of neighbouring flute grooves (enclosure README, The condenser's vents). Each reads 1.834 mm, the mullion between its two slots at the groove floor. Derek, 2026-10-21: "I am good with and I approve the vent bars."
alternatives: Wider mullions narrow the 3.1 mm slots and the open area the condenser's fan draws through; fewer slots close it further.
